{0 Variables and secrets}

The handler's {!Workero.Env.t} contains string variables, Worker secrets,
object and array variables, and resource bindings. Choose the reader by the
value's shape and the way you configure it.

{1 Read a string variable}

Set a non-sensitive value in [wrangler.toml]:

{@toml[
[vars]
GREETING = "hello"
]}

{!Workero.Env.var} returns [Some string] for a string variable or Worker
secret. It returns [None] if the name is absent or the value is not a string.
This Worker answers [hello] for [GET /] with the configuration above:

{@ocaml[
let handler _req env _ctx =
  let greeting = Option.value (Workero.Env.var env "GREETING") ~default:"unset" in
  Workero.Response.text greeting

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

Run [npx wrangler dev] and request [/]. Deploy the Worker with
[npx wrangler deploy] to use the same [vars] in Cloudflare. Do not put keys or
tokens in [vars]. See
{{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/configuration/environment-variables/}Cloudflare variables}.

{1 Require a Worker secret}

Use a Worker secret for a value that belongs to this Worker. For local
development, put [API_TOKEN=local-test-token] in [.dev.vars] beside
[wrangler.toml]. Keep [.dev.vars*] out of Git. Run [npx wrangler dev] and
request [/]; the Worker below answers [token set] without exposing the value.

{@ocaml[
let handler _req env _ctx =
  let token = Workero.Env.secret env "API_TOKEN" in
  Workero.Response.text (if token = "" then "token empty" else "token set")

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

For the deployed Worker, run [npx wrangler secret put API_TOKEN] and enter
the value at the prompt. This command creates and deploys a new Worker
version. Then deploy your code with [npx wrangler deploy].
{!Workero.Env.secret} reads the same string shape as [Env.var], but requires
it: if it is absent or not a string, it raises [Invalid_argument] with the
name and type, never the value. A local [.dev.vars] file does not upload the
secret. See {{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/configuration/secrets/}Cloudflare Worker secrets}.

{1 Read an object or array variable}

Declare a non-sensitive JSON-shaped variable in [wrangler.toml]:

{@toml[
[vars]
LIMITS = { max_items = 50, regions = ["eu", "us"] }
]}

{!Workero.Env.json} returns [Some Jsonkit.t] for an object or array. It returns
[None] for an absent value, a string, number, boolean or [null].
{!Workero.Env.var} returns [None] for these object and array values.

{@ocaml[
let handler _req env _ctx =
  match Workero.Env.json env "LIMITS" with
  | Some (`Assoc fields) -> (
      match List.assoc_opt "max_items" fields with
      | Some (`Int max_items) -> Workero.Response.text (string_of_int max_items)
      | _ ->
          Workero.Response.text ~status:`Internal_Server_Error
            "max_items must be an integer")
  | _ ->
      Workero.Response.text ~status:`Internal_Server_Error
        "LIMITS missing or not an object"

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

Run [npx wrangler dev] and request [/]; the response is [50]. The same [vars]
are sent with [npx wrangler deploy]. Add [jsonkit] to your Dune [libraries]
when using [Jsonkit] directly. {!Workero.JSON.of_js} and
{!Workero.JSON.to_js} convert general JSON-shaped JavaScript values;
{{!page-json}JSON} covers JSON HTTP bodies.

{1 Read a shared Secrets Store secret}

Secrets Store holds account-level secrets shared by Workers. Its value is a
resource binding, not a string in [env]. Configure the binding in
[wrangler.toml]:

{@toml[
[[secrets_store_secrets]]
binding = "API_KEY"
store_id = "<store id>"
secret_name = "api-key"
]}

{!Workero.Secret_store.of_env} checks the binding. {!Workero.Secret_store.get}
suspends until the value is available. It returns [None] for a [null] or
[undefined] result and raises {!Workero.Js_error} on a runtime failure.

{@ocaml[
let handler _req env _ctx =
  let key = Workero.Secret_store.of_env env "API_KEY" in
  match Workero.Secret_store.get key with
  | Some value ->
      Workero.Response.text (if value = "" then "key empty" else "key set")
  | None -> Workero.Response.text ~status:`Internal_Server_Error "key missing"

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

For local [wrangler dev], create a local store and secret, without [--remote]:

{@sh[
npx wrangler secrets-store store create local-store
npx wrangler secrets-store secret create <local-store-id> --name api-key --scopes workers
]}

Enter the secret at the prompt. Put the returned local store ID in
[store_id], run [npx wrangler dev], and request [/]. Expect [key set]. Local
development cannot read production Secrets Store secrets. To deploy, create
a remote store and secret:

{@sh[
npx wrangler secrets-store store create shared-store --remote
npx wrangler secrets-store secret create <remote-store-id> --name api-key --scopes workers --remote
]}

Put the remote store ID in [store_id], then run [npx wrangler deploy]. You
need a Secrets Store role that allows binding access. Do not put a secret
value on a command line or log it. See
{{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/secrets-store/integrations/workers/}Cloudflare Secrets Store bindings} and
{{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/wrangler/commands/}Wrangler commands}.
